WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:11.413
- Good evening.

00:00:11.413 --> 00:00:13.110
I'm happy to welcome you all to the last

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.880
of this year's Franke
Lectures in the Humanities

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.620
here at the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.600
Each year, the annual Franke
Lectures in the Humanities,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.200
are organized in conjunction
with an upper level

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.980
undergraduate humanities seminar.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.860
The lectures are made possible
by the generosity of Richard

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:30.973
and Barbara Franke,

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.430
and are intended to
present important topics

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:38.600
in the humanities to wide
and general audience.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.970
The fall 2021 Franke
Lectures in the Humanities

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.800
have been organized in conjunction

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.630
with the Yale College seminar

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.090
Mass Incarceration in the Soviet Union

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.520
and the United States.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.363
Taught by professors, Timothy
Snyder, and Jason Stanley.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.800
Tonight, we are pleased and
honored to welcome Lori Gruen.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.150
Professor Gruen is the
William Griffin Professor

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.070
of Philosophy at Wesleyan University.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.790
She is the author or editor of 15 books

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:10.510
and dozens of important
chapters and articles.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:13.100
She works at the intersection
of practical ethics

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.180
and political philosophy.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.130
Gruen is a founding faculty member

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.740
and the creator of the
Faculty Advisory Committee

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.730
of the Center for Prison
Education at Wesleyan University.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.520
She has taught incarcerated students

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.900
at Bayview Correctional Facility,

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.870
a women's prison in Chelsea,
New York now closed,

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:35.400
the Federal Correction Institution
in Danbury, Connecticut,

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.010
when it was a women's prison,

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.960
and Cheshire Correctional Institution

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.770
in Cheshire, Connecticut, a
maximum security men's prison,

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.073
and York Correctional
Institution, a women's prison.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
And today she will present
her paper on carceral logic,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.060
professor Gruen, pick it away.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.770
- Thank you so much, Professor Stanley,

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.053
I'm honored to be here.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.800
One of the things that
I'm going to do tonight

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.800
is to talk through two
really conceptual ideas,

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:09.550
carceral logics on the one hand

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.740
and an abolitionist critique on the other,

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:15.340
and I realized that for many of you,

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.840
it's the end of the semester.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.700
I understand that there's a lot going on.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.380
So I really appreciate your being here.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.173
I also made some slides
that I'm gonna share now.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:33.663
And so at least you'll
be able to follow along

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:38.533
since we don't have handouts and the like.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.710
So as Professor Stanley said,

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.860
my talk is entitled

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.863
Carceral Logics: An Abolitionist Critique.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:53.830
You may remember that
afternoon, last April 20th,

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.070
like many people across the U.S.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.260
you may have been tensely waiting to learn

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.730
what the Minneapolis jury would
decide in the murder trial

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.790
of former police officer Derek Chauvin,

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:06.193
who killed George Floyd.

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.830
Due to the pandemic we all
were able to watch the trial,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.410
and that would only have
been witnessed by family

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.410
and locals if we weren't in a pandemic.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.320
But as the judge read out the verdicts

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.160
on the three charges against Chauvin,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.160
second degree murder, guilty,
third degree murder, guilty,

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.470
and second degree manslaughter, guilty,

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.463
there was a collective
national sigh of relief,

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.240
and the reaction was emotional.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.960
Television news cutaway from
regularly scheduled programming

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.130
to broadcast some of the reactions.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.990
The Congressional Black
Caucus members for example,

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:50.020
were shown huddled together
looking at their phones

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.363
and other devices,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.670
nodding, hugging as they
listened to the verdicts.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.300
Citizens outside the courthouse

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:01.160
also watched on their phones they cheered,

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:02.650
fists were raised high,

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:07.010
many people looked up in
the heavens in gratitude.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:11.483
People held each other and
some broke down and wept.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:17.710
There was a similar, although
more muted emotional relief

00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:20.310
when the three men that
gunned down Ahmaud Arbery

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.690
were convicted a few weeks ago,

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:25.080
and a great deal of anger was generated

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.430
when Kyle Rittenhouse was found not guilty

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.670
of shooting three White men
and killing two of them,

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.873
Joseph Rosenbaum and Anthony Hoover.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:44.990
It's undeniable that many
people felt deeply gratified

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:49.150
by the carceral responses
to Chauvin's callous cruelty

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.220
and the conviction of the
men who killed Arbery.

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:56.730
So many police officers and
vigilantes are not arrested

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.270
or tried or convicted of horrible crimes

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:03.070
against Black people and
sometimes White people.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.430
And so I want you to keep that feeling

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:08.700
of wanting to punish close

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.990
while I talk about the
logics that underlie it

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:13.940
and why I think we need to break free

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:17.823
of these punitive impulses
and abolish carceral logics.

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:21.810
In this talk, I'll briefly unpack

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:25.840
and explore various ways
that carceral logics shape,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.820
excuse me, various ways
that carceral logic

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:34.133
shape our social and political relations.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.580
These harmful carceral logics, I'll argue,

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:41.610
hold us all captive and
systems of disposability,

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:45.860
permanent exclusions and
they should be abolished.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.210
But let's start first

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:51.280
by talking about how I
understand carceral logics.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.000
It has a lot of different meanings,

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.920
and I wanna be sure that
we all are on the same page

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.770
with what I think carceral logics are.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:03.580
So one way of thinking about
it is that it highlights

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:07.650
and organizes the variety
of ways that our bodies

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:12.350
and our minds and our actions
have been shaped by the idea

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:14.300
and practices of captivity

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.440
even for people who
haven't been to prison.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:22.770
And I want to just take
a moment here especially

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:25.630
to say something about
those of us who think about

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.790
and talk about prisons,
who haven't been in prison.

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.250
That is those of us who
haven't been locked up,

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:32.580
I mean, I've been in a lot of prisons

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:35.790
as Professor Stanley just
said, but that isn't important.

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:39.800
Those who are incarcerated
or formerly incarcerated

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.260
are really the best people

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:44.810
to talk about the weight
of carceral power,

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:48.620
and how they've experienced
it in their bodies and minds

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.410
and we have a lot to learn from them.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.180
I'll draw on their voices

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:56.020
and what they've taught
me throughout my talk,

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.700
but part of the reason that I'm interested

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:01.380
in talking about not
carceral power per se,

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:05.450
but carceral logics is that
it's an ideology of capture

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:08.603
and containment that impacts all of us.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.600
Those who are incarcerated,

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.800
those who are formerly incarcerated,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.853
and those of us not yet incarcerated.

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:22.660
I wanna explore what sort of thinking

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.000
these carceral logics make possible,

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.630
and perhaps more importantly,

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.890
what sorts of ideas and
practices they authorize

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:33.423
and what sorts of ideas and
practices they foreclose.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.803
I'll identify two types
of carceral logics.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:43.990
The first is a carceral
logic as understood

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:48.470
is sort of organizing, capture,
surveillance, and disposal,

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:50.520
and I'll talk a little bit about that.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:51.540
And then I'm going to turn

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.010
to a second type of carceral logic,

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:56.820
which I think of as an
ideology of domination,

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:59.790
and I'll say a little
bit about domination.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.230
And then after I talk about
these carceral logics,

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:03.770
and you get a sense of what I mean,

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:08.770
I will then turn to talking
about why we should abolish them

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:10.433
and how that might look.

00:08:12.647 --> 00:08:14.420
Okay, and I'm thinking that

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.200
most everyone in the audience today

00:08:20.753 --> 00:08:23.250
has some sense of the sort of shape

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:26.560
of the actual carceral system

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:31.000
that the sort of bricks
and mortar and razor wire.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.820
So I'm not gonna talk too much about that.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.100
As I said it's a little
bit more conceptual

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:38.500
what I'm gonna be talking about,

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.730
but when we imprison humans,

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:43.920
I'm gonna just say a
little bit now about it.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:45.470
When we in prison humans,

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.610
we harm them in ways
that are both obvious,

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:52.210
they're in conditions that
can cause physical suffering

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:56.240
and frustration and ways
that are not so obvious.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:59.290
Long-term psychological
impacts of boredom,

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:02.053
anxiety, and a lack of control.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.490
Here's how a group of incarcerated men

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:09.690
who were sentenced collectively
to a total of 225 years

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:11.720
in a maximum security prison

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:15.170
describe a slice of their experiences.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.510
And this is from a chapter

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:20.093
that they wrote in my edited volume,

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.293
the volume's called the
Ethics of Captivity.

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:29.830
And this piece that this
comes from is as you see here,

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:31.550
life behind bars.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:35.047
So what they say, and
you could read along,

00:09:35.047 --> 00:09:38.880
"After our initial humiliation
upon being strip searched

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:44.920
when we enter prison, we lose
all control of our lives.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:48.250
We will eat, bathe, and shave when told.

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:50.990
We'll be expected to follow direct orders,

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:54.020
no matter how absurd or unfair.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:57.500
Noncompliance will be met with force,

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.150
the depressive environment

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:02.180
tends to stifle intellectual curiosity.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.350
We simply become too tired

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:07.533
and Sullen to engage in
intellectual pursuits.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:11.400
Simply put, we dwell in ourselves,

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.800
we're not actively using our minds,

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:17.880
and in a very real sense,
we're thus losing our minds.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:20.760
The loneliness caused
by prisons in general

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:24.630
and prison cells in
particular is exacerbated

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:28.180
by the loss of intimacy
and the loving touch

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:30.037
of friends and family."

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.430
Those held captive are deprived so much,

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:40.100
including the ability
to make basic choices.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.510
Of course, none of us is free

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:45.620
to make any kind of choices we might want.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.700
We're restricted in all sorts of ways

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:49.730
by the genders that were assigned,

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:52.910
by our physical and cognitive
abilities, by class,

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:57.803
by religion, by race, by
ethnicity, even by our species.

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:00.800
We're restricted by state power

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:03.020
and other governmental
and economic institutions

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:05.170
that prescribe all sorts of choices

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.313
and force others upon us.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:12.300
Carceral prison spaces
are particularly stark

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.330
in their ways of limiting

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:22.590
and reducing the choices
that are available

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:25.330
for those who are within them,

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:29.200
cages and cells and iron
doors, little natural light,

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:34.200
concrete and steel, razor
wire, and metallic noise.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.000
And these carceral spaces
are designed in part

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:44.490
and organized by a carceral logic

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:48.483
that makes these choices impossible.

00:11:50.462 --> 00:11:53.040
But there are carceral spaces
that function beyond prisons

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:58.040
and Gulags and those also
control and contain others,

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:01.540
immigration detention centers, zoos,

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:04.910
laboratories, psychiatric institutions,

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:08.610
even some senior living places
are sites of containment,

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:10.523
surveillance, and control.

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.963
They're also often sites of undoing.

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:18.660
The carceral spaces
are not simply punitive

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:21.880
and they do not merely serve as Fuko noted

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:24.990
as socializing and
disciplining institutions

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:26.960
and to sites of surveillance.

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:30.660
Increasingly we're seeing
that they also function

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:34.170
to reinforce a permanent state of injury.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:36.920
And part of that injury
is the disposability

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:39.563
of those who occupy those spaces.

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:43.600
Some humans have always fallen

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:48.020
into the category of
disposable or fungible.

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:49.490
People stolen from Africa

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:52.550
and the transatlantic slave
trade are prime examples,

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:55.090
but there are an estimated
27 million people

00:12:55.090 --> 00:12:57.190
in slavery today.

00:12:57.190 --> 00:12:59.930
Kevin Bales in his book,
"Disposable People,"

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:03.597
conservatively estimates
that a larger, sorry,

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:08.340
a larger number of people
than the whole population

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:11.780
of Canada are currently enslaved.

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:12.870
The bulk of these people,

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:15.000
approximately 15 to 20 million,

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.700
are in conditions of
bounded labor in India,

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.640
Pakistan, Bangladesh, and Nepal.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:23.710
They become enslaved as
security against alone,

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:25.720
or when they inherited debt.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.150
In other words, these
persons have a price.

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:30.990
I won't go into cons at this moment,

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:34.870
but if those of you who are
aware of con will now recognize

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:39.870
that these disposable beings
with a price are mirror things.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:43.700
And then when they don't
work to their capacity,

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:44.913
they're disposed of.

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:49.950
When you're disposable,
you have lesser status.

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:54.330
Sometimes the status is
the status of a thing,

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:57.570
and this isn't only a
slight or an affront,

00:13:57.570 --> 00:14:01.350
but a way of reminding both
the one being denied the status

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:02.870
and others around

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:07.563
that one is in the category
deemed disposable abject other.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:10.840
That's one way that the carceral logics

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:13.103
that I'm worried about operate.

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:21.600
The other kind of logic, as I mentioned,

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.223
is one of domination.

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:28.670
There are at least two ways
that we can think of domination.

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:29.830
One that supports

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:33.480
and maintains not just
disciplinary control of bodies,

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.850
but taxonomies of power,

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:40.470
the other upholds ontological
exclusions or outsideness

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:43.150
and the violence it authorizes.

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:44.780
The first sort of domination

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:47.410
is characterized by its justicetory power

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.390
upholding oppressive institutions,

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:52.520
relationships, and practices.

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:56.730
Domination according to political
philosopher Philip Pettit

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:58.610
occurs when a person is in a position

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:03.610
in which someone has the
power to arbitrarily interfere

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:07.133
with choices they would
otherwise have been able to make.

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:11.930
And this notion of domination
is pretty expansive

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:14.810
because the interference
just needs to be possible

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:16.010
in the form of a threat.

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:19.000
It has wide reaching ramifications

00:15:19.986 --> 00:15:21.730
and that one can internalize

00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:25.240
the potential to be the
victim of domination

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:28.540
and one can imagine one
has the power to dominate

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:31.900
and so act as if it's their privilege,

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:34.750
and this way of thinking naturalizes

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:37.093
or normalizes domination.

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:41.340
Remedying this sort of carceral logic

00:15:42.740 --> 00:15:45.770
is important in many
contexts when the dominator

00:15:45.770 --> 00:15:49.410
and the dominated exist on the same plane

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:52.050
when they are at the
same table as it were.

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:53.940
What I'm thinking of here is that

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:57.120
the relationship of
dominated and dominator

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:00.563
or the dominator and the submissive,

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:06.120
this may be a relationship
in which both are recognized

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:09.330
as having a capacity to switch roles.

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:12.400
And the roles are just at
this particular juncture

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:15.670
because of particular
political and social,

00:16:15.670 --> 00:16:19.090
and probably historical
features of the situation

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:23.760
end up having that divergent
relationship of domination

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:28.760
and submission but it's a
kind of fixable domination

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:31.390
as it were.

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:33.200
If you think about the example also,

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:35.513
I was thinking teachers and students,

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:41.650
there's a certain relation of
power there, but domination,

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:46.300
but like overt domination
that's objectionable domination,

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:48.140
not just the relationship of power,

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:53.140
but the objectively problematic domination

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:57.340
can be called out by the student.

00:16:57.340 --> 00:17:00.480
Similarly, students have
certain amount of power.

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:04.280
They can also be called out by a teacher

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:06.660
about their abuse of that power.

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:10.670
So what I'm getting at here is
that this particular version

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:15.670
of domination sort of recognized
the possibility of remedy,

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:20.460
but there's a deeper type of injury

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:23.693
associated with carceral
logics of domination.

00:17:24.820 --> 00:17:28.913
For whom domination is a
condition of existence.

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:31.060
This is what I meant earlier

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:33.913
when I said it has an ontological flavor.

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:40.890
The idea here is that there's
a category, the slave,

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:43.140
the prisoner, the animal,

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:48.140
and their category is
fundamentally lower status.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:51.483
There's no way of to this
domination relationship.

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:54.913
It's the condition of existence.

00:17:56.670 --> 00:18:01.670
This carceral logic that
these categorical individuals

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:07.160
are subjected to go as beyond
this first sort of domination.

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:11.280
Here is a logic that serves
as a conceptual mechanism

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:15.580
that fixes and solidifies exclusions.

00:18:15.580 --> 00:18:19.023
It permanently marks another as an other.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:21.710
This sort of carceral logic

00:18:21.710 --> 00:18:25.193
is especially obvious in
anti-Black racism, for example.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:30.620
Okay, so this was my brief discussion

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:34.113
of two types of carceral logic
that I'm concerned about.

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:39.300
We can talk more, I hope we will later,

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:42.380
but these carceral logics organize

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:44.570
our systems of captivity for humans

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:47.620
and I should add also for animals

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:49.370
and they structure domination

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:52.210
that consistently denies many humans

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:54.823
the very chance of being free.

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:59.680
Carceral logics have
also colonized our minds

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:04.550
making it hard to imagine
how we can make any changes.

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:07.790
This is why we have the emotions we have

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:10.110
when Chauvin was found guilty

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:14.790
and why we more often have the
disappointments that we have

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:17.540
when many of those in
power are not charged

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:21.260
or tried or convicted when they
act with egregious violence

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:22.863
against the powerless.

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:25.820
I have those two even
though I've been thinking

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:27.710
about these issues for a really long time,

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:29.280
I still have that.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:32.220
It's not possible to completely escape

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:33.830
the way in which carceral logics

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:36.520
have colonized our thinking.

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:39.720
It is really hard to get
out of it in other words,

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:43.120
but I think we can abolish
these carceral logics

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:46.410
from our thinking and our
feeling and our acting.

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:50.240
And we can work at least
to free our imaginations

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:52.190
and create the ground to stand

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:54.453
and think against such logics.

00:19:55.420 --> 00:20:00.420
And one way we can do that is
by thinking about abolition

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:03.440
and abolition has a lot of different,

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:07.010
again, like carceral logics
abolition is a big category.

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:08.330
There's a lot of different ways

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:10.400
of understanding what it means.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:13.330
Many people think of it
in terms of the abolition

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:17.450
of prisons themselves,

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:20.470
I'm thinking of it more in this context

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:25.010
as a way of getting
outside of carceral logic.

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:26.983
So abolishing carceral logic,

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:30.080
and I'll talk a little
bit about the history

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.920
of abolition as a notion as well,

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:35.200
but I want us to sort of put aside

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:37.090
the notion of abolishing prisons

00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:41.980
even though that is not
consistent with what I'm saying,

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:44.010
but it's not the focus
of what I'm talking about

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:46.030
when I'm talking about abolition.

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:50.210
So abolition as I understand
it in this discussion

00:20:50.210 --> 00:20:54.970
is a framework for
reimagining our own agency

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:57.700
for reenvisioning our communities

00:20:57.700 --> 00:21:00.493
and for reframing our social
and political relations.

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:04.520
In a fairly recent, I think
last year or the year before

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:07.560
New York Times article about geographer

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:09.810
and abolitionists Ruthie Gilmore,

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:12.817
law professor James Forman
Jr. said that quote,

00:21:12.817 --> 00:21:16.400
"What I love about abolition
and now using my own thinking,

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:19.200
and when I identify
myself as an abolitionist,

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:20.430
this is what I have in mind

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:24.020
is that the idea that you
imagine a world without prisons,

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:28.570
and then you work to build
that world, or in my terms,

00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:31.720
you imagine a world
without carceral logic,

00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:34.040
and then you work to build that world."

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:37.383
So abolition is aspirational.

00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:42.520
Imagining otherwise moving
against carceral logic,

00:21:42.520 --> 00:21:44.390
we'll be trying,

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:47.840
we're asked to think in
fundamentally different ways,

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:51.620
thinking that's contextual,
relational, historical,

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:54.200
maybe even poetic with a little bit

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:58.150
of what Devonya Havis a
philosopher describes as alchemy

00:21:58.150 --> 00:22:02.613
will need ingenuity to
negotiate what seems impossible.

00:22:03.610 --> 00:22:06.920
So before getting into how we
might move in the direction

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:11.567
of imagining how to
negotiate impossibility

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:13.930
or what seems impossible,

00:22:13.930 --> 00:22:16.653
lemme say a little bit about
the history of abolition.

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:22.900
So the term abolition is
best known, of course,

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:26.413
in the context of opposition
to the Atlantic slave trade.

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:33.120
Early abolitionists were opposed
to the violent accumulation

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:36.460
of wealth due to forced labor.

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:39.360
According to Manisha Sinha

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:43.743
in her book, "The Slave's Cause"

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:47.960
abolition is though primarily concerned

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:52.670
with resisting slavery
had wide reaching concerns

00:22:52.670 --> 00:22:54.800
and I'll leave this up so you can see

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:57.893
what she thinks as also
being really important,

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:03.210
and a part of what it
was that abolitionists,

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:05.913
the early oven abolitionists were doing.

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:12.890
So with the abolition of
slavery after the civil war,

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:16.660
many abolitionists were
actively reimagining

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:20.680
how to bring the ideals of
democracy into fuller practice.

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:24.010
So democracy was a big commitment

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:26.370
of these early abolitionists

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:31.100
and the abolitionists after
including importantly,

00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:34.800
sociologists W. E. B. Du
Bois in Black Reconstruction

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:37.200
noted that abolition democracy

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:39.910
demanded the structural reform of labor,

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:41.910
education, and opportunities.

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:45.540
Now notice, so, we're going
from a thought of abolition

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:52.520
of slavery and forced labor practices

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:55.240
to a different way of thinking

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:59.990
about how to organize our communities

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:01.800
and the opportunities

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:04.500
and resources that are available
within those communities.

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:06.210
So this is important point.

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:10.100
It wasn't enough to emancipate
Black people from slavery,

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:12.180
many envisioned building a free

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:16.290
and equal society in which
the disadvantages that emerged

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:19.183
from the system of slavery
were not permanent.

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:22.780
But as I was suggesting,

00:24:22.780 --> 00:24:25.430
the second version of carceral logics

00:24:25.430 --> 00:24:28.110
has a kind of afterlife.

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:31.540
And that afterlife is
something that we see

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:35.713
at the end of reconstruction as well.

00:24:36.670 --> 00:24:39.470
And the reimplementation
of the Black codes,

00:24:39.470 --> 00:24:43.640
which perpetuated forms of
unfreedom for Black people.

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:46.360
For example, they could be prosecuted

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:48.440
for not carrying proof of employment,

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:52.120
which led to incarceration
and with forced labor.

00:24:52.120 --> 00:24:53.460
So in the South,

00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:57.760
what this mounted to was
a convict leasing program

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:00.890
and the growing population
of Black prisoners

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:06.870
ended up going back to work without pay.

00:25:08.477 --> 00:25:12.670
And it was an extraordinarily
abusive system.

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:15.870
Du Bois quotes from a
Southern White woman,

00:25:15.870 --> 00:25:19.923
and this is what she says,

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:23.550
in some states where convict labor is sold

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:26.120
to the highest bidder the cruel treatment

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:28.920
of the helpless human chattel
in the hands of guards

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:32.623
is such as no tongue can
tell nor pen picture.

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:36.070
Prison inspectors find
convicts herded together

00:25:36.070 --> 00:25:37.610
irrespective of the age

00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:39.490
and there were a lot
of children, actually,

00:25:39.490 --> 00:25:41.290
a lot of children.

00:25:41.290 --> 00:25:43.290
Confined at night in shackles,

00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:48.290
housed sometimes as has
been found in old box cars

00:25:48.950 --> 00:25:52.650
packed almost as closely
as sardines in a box.

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:55.300
During the day all are
worked under armed guards

00:25:55.300 --> 00:25:57.170
who stand ready to shoot down

00:25:57.170 --> 00:26:00.860
any who may attempt to escape
from this hell upon earth

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:02.943
the modern American Bastille.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:07.580
Should one escape the bloodhounds
trained for the purpose

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:09.610
are put upon his tracks

00:26:09.610 --> 00:26:12.330
and the chances are that
he will be brought back

00:26:12.330 --> 00:26:16.643
severely flogged and put in
double shackles or worse.

00:26:21.620 --> 00:26:24.100
As this shocking conditions
of convict leasing

00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:27.750
became widely known pressure
built to end the practice

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:32.250
and one by one Southern
states didn't renew the,

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:34.550
or initiate the leases.

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:39.513
Alabama was the final state to
end convict leasing in 1928.

00:26:40.580 --> 00:26:44.450
Okay, fast forward, I'm a
philosopher not historian, sorry.

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:46.510
Fast forward to 1970s.

00:26:46.510 --> 00:26:50.613
So 1928 to 1970s lots happened,
but we'll just fast forward.

00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:55.660
There were a new form of abolition emerged

00:26:55.660 --> 00:27:00.310
and that was led a lot by Quakers,

00:27:00.310 --> 00:27:05.310
and it was a form of
abolition of against prisons.

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:09.460
And importantly, it's 1976.

00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:14.460
So, you know that mass
incarceration wasn't really full on

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:17.000
in that period,

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:20.373
but there were nonetheless
prison abolitionists.

00:27:21.830 --> 00:27:25.470
The first chapter of a
book that was put together

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:29.263
by Fay Honey Knopp called
"Instead of Prisons"

00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:33.723
with a group of Quakers
and other political actors,

00:27:34.900 --> 00:27:36.253
in the first chapter,

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:41.840
they have comments from lawyers
and judges and prisoners

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:45.670
and those employed or formerly
employed by prisons, authors,

00:27:45.670 --> 00:27:49.023
activists, and many who condemns prisons,

00:27:50.420 --> 00:27:53.040
and these are just some of the examples.

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:55.420
And I think it's really quite striking

00:27:55.420 --> 00:28:00.220
how a judge, a professor at Columbia,

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:04.150
a former psychiatrist at Sing Sing

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:09.150
all were in favor of
abolishing the prisons.

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:11.970
And again, this is abolishing prisons

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:16.563
prior to our current era
of mass incarceration.

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:24.023
Okay, that's my brief historical tour.

00:28:26.320 --> 00:28:28.490
Now you may have some concerns

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:33.490
and reactions about the idea
of abolition most people do.

00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:38.820
It invokes a variety of
different kinds of feelings

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:43.820
like incredulity and disdain
to curiosity and excitement.

00:28:44.030 --> 00:28:46.580
And these reactions also
vary depending on the context

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:50.590
in which the topic of
abolition is being raised

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:52.820
and the ways that it's being discussed.

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:56.820
So I wanna give you this
recent example from 2017,

00:28:56.820 --> 00:29:01.820
the ACLU conducted a survey
that found that 61% of Americans

00:29:02.830 --> 00:29:05.760
believe that people who
suffer from drug addiction

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:09.440
and commit serious crimes
don't belong in prison,

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:11.293
but should receive treatment.

00:29:12.410 --> 00:29:15.680
And 87% believe that when people

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:20.080
with mental health disabilities
commit serious crimes

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:22.050
that involve violence,

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:24.410
they should be sent to
mental health programs

00:29:24.410 --> 00:29:27.300
where they can receive
treatment from professionals.

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:28.353
So not to prison.

00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:32.940
So this is recent, and
this is a huge majority

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:36.910
of the U.S. population.

00:29:36.910 --> 00:29:39.600
Such strong public support

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:43.870
for these non-carceral
responses to violent crimes

00:29:43.870 --> 00:29:47.190
are I think really important to note

00:29:47.190 --> 00:29:50.143
in a country that locks
up over 2 million people,

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:52.660
and as feminist scholar

00:29:52.660 --> 00:29:55.530
and abolitionists Joy James has noted

00:29:55.530 --> 00:29:59.270
abolitionists alternatives to
prisons are already available

00:29:59.270 --> 00:30:01.420
for those with wealth and power.

00:30:01.420 --> 00:30:04.090
She writes, existential whiteness,

00:30:04.090 --> 00:30:07.450
money, or capital and
connections to governing elites

00:30:07.450 --> 00:30:09.580
allow offenders if prosecuted

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:14.350
and convicted to be largely
redirected to therapy,

00:30:14.350 --> 00:30:16.310
counseling, drug treatment,

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:18.943
and expensive residential
treatment centers,

00:30:21.250 --> 00:30:25.440
but abolishing prisons is
only one way as I've suggested

00:30:25.440 --> 00:30:27.890
of thinking about abolition.

00:30:27.890 --> 00:30:29.900
I think it's an important project.

00:30:29.900 --> 00:30:32.910
Don't get me wrong, I'm
involved in that project,

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:35.150
however, tonight I'm really thinking

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:37.833
of abolishing carceral logics,

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:43.130
and that is consistent as I
said with abolishing prisons,

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:47.870
but I think I'm more interested tonight

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:52.790
in talking about how we
can end our acceptance

00:30:52.790 --> 00:30:55.610
of punitive responses to crime

00:30:55.610 --> 00:30:59.866
and other acts that are
outside of the accepted norms

00:30:59.866 --> 00:31:03.880
and to begin imagining alternatives

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:05.573
to these carceral logics.

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:08.590
In a society like ours

00:31:08.590 --> 00:31:11.560
that's organized by
profound and inequality,

00:31:11.560 --> 00:31:13.900
abolitionists are working
to develop more just

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:15.930
and meaningful social policy

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:18.620
that will help support community needs

00:31:18.620 --> 00:31:22.053
and build alternative humane institutions.

00:31:23.890 --> 00:31:28.890
These efforts involve
promoting non-reformist reforms

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:34.250
that are, I think, a really important idea

00:31:35.300 --> 00:31:39.620
that allow for the development of policies

00:31:39.620 --> 00:31:42.513
and practices that minimize harm,

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:47.530
and also at the same
time don't contribute to,

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:50.423
or reinforce carceral logics.

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:53.720
So let me just give you some examples

00:31:56.998 --> 00:31:59.930
of what I think are non-reformist reforms,

00:31:59.930 --> 00:32:02.750
we can discuss this further
if this is something

00:32:02.750 --> 00:32:07.750
that you either disagree with
or don't think makes sense,

00:32:08.290 --> 00:32:10.640
but the program, the Center
for Prison Education,

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:12.980
which is at Wesleyan is a program

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:15.600
that I've been involved
in from the beginning

00:32:17.756 --> 00:32:22.020
and also the newer, but also
quite robust program at Yale,

00:32:22.020 --> 00:32:24.620
the old Prison Education Initiative

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:27.940
are both what I would call
non-reformist reforms.

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:29.770
What do I mean by that?

00:32:29.770 --> 00:32:33.210
I think that part of what
we should be understanding

00:32:33.210 --> 00:32:35.850
when we're thinking about
non-reformist reforms

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:40.850
is ways in which some of the
logic of the carceral logic

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:46.600
and the implications of
carceral logic can be undone,

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:48.520
but the prison remains

00:32:48.520 --> 00:32:51.070
and the education is happening in prison.

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:52.980
I'm not advocating that
the prisoner remains,

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:55.210
but I'm suggesting that in the moment

00:32:55.210 --> 00:32:56.923
when the prison exists,

00:32:59.470 --> 00:33:02.940
that it's possible to provide
educational opportunities

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:05.470
that allow those people
who are incarcerated

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:07.770
and able to enroll in various classes

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:12.480
to be able to get there
opportunities that they didn't have

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:13.990
to be exposed to ideas

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:17.930
that they didn't actually
weren't exposed to prior

00:33:17.930 --> 00:33:20.310
that they're also able
to develop their own ways

00:33:20.310 --> 00:33:25.240
of thinking about themselves
in relationship to others,

00:33:25.240 --> 00:33:26.640
in relationship to the state,

00:33:26.640 --> 00:33:30.720
in relationship to our fragile democracy

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:32.990
or non-existent democracy

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:36.330
in relation to all sorts of
social and political structures.

00:33:36.330 --> 00:33:39.560
So why it's a non-reformist reform

00:33:39.560 --> 00:33:43.610
because even though those
of us who do this work

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:49.320
have to abide by the carceral rules

00:33:49.320 --> 00:33:53.040
of the departments of correction,

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:56.180
we can work within those systems,

00:33:56.180 --> 00:34:01.180
within the first version of
carceral logics to undermine

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:05.620
the very sort of strategies
of carceral logics,

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:07.910
to undermine the very attitudes

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:10.460
that are part of the carceral logic.

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:12.900
I've also included the
Freedom Reads project,

00:34:12.900 --> 00:34:15.790
which is another I think
non-reformist reform,

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:17.770
putting freedom libraries,

00:34:17.770 --> 00:34:21.740
micro libraries of 500 books into prisons

00:34:21.740 --> 00:34:23.123
all across the country,

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:29.530
and I also included here on this slide

00:34:29.530 --> 00:34:34.530
some ideas about mutual aid
that has become really important

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:36.670
in the context of the pandemic,

00:34:36.670 --> 00:34:39.030
where communities are working together

00:34:39.030 --> 00:34:43.270
to help each other outside
of the various systems

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:46.040
that are confining and containing

00:34:46.040 --> 00:34:48.523
and have colonized our thinking.

00:34:49.740 --> 00:34:53.960
So part of the mutual aid process

00:34:53.960 --> 00:34:57.440
has involved redirecting local budgets

00:34:57.440 --> 00:35:02.440
to help those who are food
insecure or housing insecure,

00:35:04.893 --> 00:35:07.450
or vulnerable to substance use

00:35:07.450 --> 00:35:10.999
developing opportunities to support youth,

00:35:10.999 --> 00:35:15.760
to creating community-based
harm reduction programs,

00:35:15.760 --> 00:35:17.860
working on supporting victims of harm

00:35:17.860 --> 00:35:20.470
through restorative justice practices

00:35:20.470 --> 00:35:23.060
and holding those who
caused harm accountable

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:27.640
in a non-carceral way as well
as other practical efforts

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:30.170
to build a more caring world.

00:35:30.170 --> 00:35:32.150
And these sorts of community programs

00:35:32.150 --> 00:35:35.410
I think are extraordinarily meaningful

00:35:35.410 --> 00:35:39.350
because they both allow
community accountability,

00:35:39.350 --> 00:35:44.120
but then also allow us to
reimagine our social relations

00:35:44.120 --> 00:35:47.163
outside of these carceral logics.

00:35:49.730 --> 00:35:51.230
Many of you may be thinking,

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:56.230
at least I would have thought
this at one point in my life

00:35:56.310 --> 00:35:57.810
that this is all utopian.

00:35:57.810 --> 00:36:02.787
This is a kind of utopian notion,

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:08.450
and in some ways I do think that's true.

00:36:08.450 --> 00:36:11.910
I think that it may very well be utopian,

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:14.620
but I'm trying to reframe
my thinking about utopia,

00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:16.060
and I'll say a bit about that in a sec,

00:36:16.060 --> 00:36:16.940
but this is a really,

00:36:16.940 --> 00:36:19.260
I think, important insight that I learned

00:36:19.260 --> 00:36:22.257
from a philosopher William
Paris who writes that,

00:36:22.257 --> 00:36:24.950
"Utopias have been attempts to carve

00:36:24.950 --> 00:36:26.940
an alternative space of life

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:29.160
that was sufficiently disconnected

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:31.610
from the dominant social order.

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:34.100
Utopian need not concern itself

00:36:34.100 --> 00:36:36.490
with designing a perfect life.

00:36:36.490 --> 00:36:39.040
Instead, utopia may take up the task

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:42.990
of creating social spaces
that allow for deliberation

00:36:43.950 --> 00:36:46.830
over different ideas about
how we might organize

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:48.700
our social relations.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:52.940
Utopia is the creation of
an alternative public sphere

00:36:52.940 --> 00:36:55.220
for testing out ideas

00:36:55.220 --> 00:37:00.010
and disagreements over
novel forms of social life."

00:37:00.010 --> 00:37:05.010
So in this sense, I think
all social theorizing

00:37:05.210 --> 00:37:07.513
might be thought to be utopian.

00:37:09.870 --> 00:37:12.120
Confinement, captivity,

00:37:12.120 --> 00:37:14.500
and carceral logics that
I've been discussing

00:37:14.500 --> 00:37:18.430
are so central to our social imagination

00:37:18.430 --> 00:37:20.830
that it's challenging
to think beyond them.

00:37:20.830 --> 00:37:24.060
An utopian thinking is
what will help unsettle

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:26.790
our imaginations so we can start to expand

00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:29.340
the horizons of possibility.

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:31.540
To move beyond carceral logics

00:37:31.540 --> 00:37:36.190
and their punitive
manifestations in practice

00:37:36.190 --> 00:37:40.587
we need what abolitionist
Erica Meiner calls for quote

00:37:40.587 --> 00:37:43.420
"a jailbreak of the imagination"

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:45.993
in order to make the impossible possible.

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:50.270
Utopian thinking can free the imagination

00:37:50.270 --> 00:37:51.870
from the constraints that the violent,

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:54.280
oppressive, and limiting social conditions

00:37:54.280 --> 00:37:56.260
that currently exist in the U.S.

00:37:56.260 --> 00:37:59.550
but such thinking is not
only aimed at some hope

00:37:59.550 --> 00:38:01.560
for a peaceful future.

00:38:01.560 --> 00:38:05.040
By imagining otherwise we
are being asked to think

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:08.200
in fundamentally different new ways.

00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:10.560
And one of the new ways
I'd like to talk about

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:14.640
as I wind down my discussion
tonight is what I'm calling

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:17.163
an abolitionist ethic of care.

00:38:19.394 --> 00:38:21.410
An abolitionist ethic of care

00:38:21.410 --> 00:38:25.350
would focus on the particularities
of caring relationships

00:38:25.350 --> 00:38:27.920
informed by differences in context

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:30.550
as well as the racial,
economic, ethnic, cultural,

00:38:30.550 --> 00:38:33.720
and differently gendered
experiences of individuals

00:38:33.720 --> 00:38:36.270
and those that care for in community.

00:38:36.270 --> 00:38:39.100
An ethics of care I think is well suited

00:38:39.100 --> 00:38:42.013
to be the basis of an
abolitionists practice.

00:38:43.670 --> 00:38:46.850
Care ethics in more general terms

00:38:46.850 --> 00:38:49.140
allows us to formulate
better understandings

00:38:49.140 --> 00:38:50.990
of ethical problems.

00:38:50.990 --> 00:38:54.790
We turn to ethical theories
in conditions of conflict,

00:38:54.790 --> 00:38:56.630
generally all ethical theories

00:38:56.630 --> 00:39:00.090
to gain insights into how
we might find solutions

00:39:00.090 --> 00:39:03.390
hard to solve social and political issues.

00:39:03.390 --> 00:39:06.910
An ethic of care addresses
situations of conflict,

00:39:06.910 --> 00:39:09.330
of course, but also is concerned

00:39:09.330 --> 00:39:11.450
with how people come
to see a moral problem

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:13.810
as a problem in the first place,

00:39:13.810 --> 00:39:16.170
and works to develop moral imagination,

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:18.893
not only as a way of reframing problems,

00:39:19.760 --> 00:39:22.050
but also as a way to
move towards more just

00:39:22.050 --> 00:39:26.053
in settled standable solutions
to those kinds of problems.

00:39:29.820 --> 00:39:32.990
In the exercise of moral imagination,

00:39:32.990 --> 00:39:37.630
care ethics is attentive
to differences in power,

00:39:37.630 --> 00:39:40.280
as well as the cultural underpinnings

00:39:40.280 --> 00:39:45.170
of systems of exploitation,
commodification, and cruelty.

00:39:45.170 --> 00:39:47.880
By analyzing the specific context

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:50.450
in which systems of power operate

00:39:50.450 --> 00:39:52.220
care ethics is concerned about

00:39:52.220 --> 00:39:54.400
the larger structures that allow

00:39:54.400 --> 00:39:58.920
and contribute to various forms
of injustice and inequality.

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:01.400
Care ethics also directs our attention

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:04.150
to the specific particular people

00:40:04.150 --> 00:40:05.720
that are involved in the conflict

00:40:05.720 --> 00:40:10.280
and in so doing helps us
to reframe our concerns.

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:14.963
It encourages the development
of empathy and compassion.

00:40:17.600 --> 00:40:20.650
But care is also a capacity
that has to be nurtured

00:40:21.910 --> 00:40:24.960
or cared for, care needs to be nurtured

00:40:24.960 --> 00:40:26.610
and cared for itself.

00:40:26.610 --> 00:40:29.910
And carceral logics another
feature of carceral logic

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:32.430
is they detach us from care

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:35.523
and they promote that sort of detachment,

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:39.450
and abolitionists ethic
would involve reimagining

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:43.573
our caring relations and
encourage and deepen them.

00:40:44.630 --> 00:40:47.010
It also requires an analysis

00:40:47.010 --> 00:40:50.550
of the impacts of failures of care.

00:40:50.550 --> 00:40:53.470
Both before carceral solutions are sought

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:55.160
when harms are committed

00:40:55.160 --> 00:40:59.020
and in critiquing the
dehumanizing conditions

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:01.873
that exist in carceral spaces.

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:08.453
An abolitionist ethic of
care can help both victims,

00:41:09.400 --> 00:41:14.010
and those who caused harm
reframe their experiences.

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:17.840
Part of the solution to
seemingly intractable conflict

00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:22.840
is to care for oneself
in relations of respect

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:25.630
and also self-respect.

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:29.080
As feminist thinker Sara Ahmed notes,

00:41:29.080 --> 00:41:29.960
and here she's drawing

00:41:29.960 --> 00:41:33.223
on another feminist thinker Audre Lorde,

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:35.703
work, this is a quote,

00:41:36.747 --> 00:41:40.680
"The work of self care is about
the creation of community,

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:44.030
fragile communities assembled
out of the experiences

00:41:44.030 --> 00:41:45.183
of being shattered.

00:41:46.560 --> 00:41:49.780
We reassemble ourselves
through the ordinary everyday

00:41:49.780 --> 00:41:53.630
and often painstaking work
of looking after ourselves

00:41:53.630 --> 00:41:57.355
and looking after each other." And quote.

00:41:57.355 --> 00:41:59.880
And ethics of care is not meant to be

00:41:59.880 --> 00:42:02.440
sort of condescending intervention

00:42:02.440 --> 00:42:04.650
where care covers over pity

00:42:04.650 --> 00:42:07.250
or some equally contemptuous attitude,

00:42:07.250 --> 00:42:10.520
but rather it's an ethic that
works to create the conditions

00:42:10.520 --> 00:42:12.340
in which caring for oneself

00:42:12.340 --> 00:42:15.493
and for others are developed
practice and valued.

00:42:16.530 --> 00:42:18.963
It's restorative and transformative work.

00:42:22.320 --> 00:42:25.130
Carceral logics lead us
to misguided attempts

00:42:25.130 --> 00:42:29.030
to try to bring about
some sort of justice.

00:42:29.030 --> 00:42:31.340
As I've tried to suggest tonight,

00:42:31.340 --> 00:42:33.840
there are alternatives
to this way of thinking

00:42:33.840 --> 00:42:36.200
and feeling that inflicts more harm

00:42:36.200 --> 00:42:37.210
and does very little

00:42:37.210 --> 00:42:39.790
to fundamentally change people's attitude

00:42:39.790 --> 00:42:42.533
towards the harm caused by these logics.

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:45.410
Abolitionists have been working to develop

00:42:45.410 --> 00:42:47.890
ways of reimagining our relationships

00:42:47.890 --> 00:42:49.520
and our responses to harm

00:42:49.520 --> 00:42:53.910
that don't involve
punitive carceral actions.

00:42:53.910 --> 00:42:56.200
An abolitionist ethic of care

00:42:56.200 --> 00:42:59.900
is a promising area for
revaluing those who are harmed,

00:42:59.900 --> 00:43:01.013
those who cause harm,

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:04.430
and the broader community who are impacted

00:43:04.430 --> 00:43:07.430
not just by the pain of harms
that have been committed,

00:43:07.430 --> 00:43:08.970
but by the harms that are inflicted

00:43:08.970 --> 00:43:10.543
by the state in their name.

00:43:11.950 --> 00:43:16.810
Caring, empathetic,
reimagining is what we need

00:43:16.810 --> 00:43:19.280
to escape carceral logics,

00:43:19.280 --> 00:43:23.950
and it's crucial to promoting
and achieving freedom.

00:43:23.950 --> 00:43:25.300
Thanks.

00:43:25.300 --> 00:43:27.263
I look forward to this discussion.

00:43:30.370 --> 00:43:34.200
- Thank you, Professor
Gruen. That that was great.

00:43:34.200 --> 00:43:39.200
Now, so please place your
questions in the Q and A,

00:43:41.806 --> 00:43:46.677
and I'll go through
them at moderate debate.

00:43:53.530 --> 00:43:56.313
So, let's begin, I guess,

00:43:58.080 --> 00:44:02.417
so let's begin first with
one question which is about,

00:44:14.360 --> 00:44:16.160
can you connect this to your work?

00:44:16.160 --> 00:44:20.100
So you're a theorist of
the ethics of captivity.

00:44:20.100 --> 00:44:25.000
One of the questions
saw I think very clearly

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:30.000
the link between your arguments
and your position on ethics.

00:44:32.570 --> 00:44:36.250
Do you wanna talk about
that link and expand on it?

00:44:36.250 --> 00:44:38.520
- Yeah, thanks for asking about that.

00:44:38.520 --> 00:44:41.830
So there's work that I,

00:44:41.830 --> 00:44:45.940
you heard me say a little
bit throughout the discussion

00:44:47.550 --> 00:44:49.340
that I'm interested in thinking about this

00:44:49.340 --> 00:44:51.340
in terms of animals as well,

00:44:51.340 --> 00:44:53.640
and the ways in which species itself

00:44:53.640 --> 00:44:57.160
might be a type of carceral logic.

00:44:57.160 --> 00:45:01.980
And so importantly, the work
that I was thinking about

00:45:04.030 --> 00:45:06.300
I started to realize and
this is very dangerous

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:07.980
and I'm well aware of the dangers.

00:45:07.980 --> 00:45:10.900
And so I want to just highlight caveat

00:45:10.900 --> 00:45:12.660
that these are not comparisons

00:45:12.660 --> 00:45:16.690
between animals in captive
environments say zoos

00:45:16.690 --> 00:45:19.290
and humans in prisons or Gulags

00:45:19.290 --> 00:45:22.820
or other kinds of carceral enclosure.

00:45:22.820 --> 00:45:26.940
So I'm not making a sort of
simplistic comparison here,

00:45:26.940 --> 00:45:30.870
but I do think that the
underlying, the background ideas,

00:45:30.870 --> 00:45:35.870
the logic that allows for the
kinds of things I talked about

00:45:36.170 --> 00:45:41.090
disposability, incapacity, disrespect,

00:45:41.090 --> 00:45:43.750
lack of value, permanent injury.

00:45:43.750 --> 00:45:45.890
Those are the kinds of things that I think

00:45:45.890 --> 00:45:48.010
are part of the conceptualization.

00:45:48.010 --> 00:45:50.200
That's a feature of the carceral logic

00:45:50.200 --> 00:45:53.910
that allows for an is sort of before

00:45:53.910 --> 00:45:57.260
prior to the existence
of these carceral spaces.

00:45:57.260 --> 00:45:59.430
And so I think there's a very tight link

00:45:59.430 --> 00:46:04.430
between the ways in which we
imagine or humans imagine,

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:08.670
some humans imagine themselves
as being in a position

00:46:08.670 --> 00:46:11.100
of just clear domination

00:46:11.100 --> 00:46:15.010
and some scala naturae
about we're just higher up

00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:17.460
than these other animals.

00:46:17.460 --> 00:46:21.850
And similarly, and importantly,
the sort of racist attitudes

00:46:21.850 --> 00:46:24.328
are very similar that
they're somehow higher,

00:46:24.328 --> 00:46:26.503
the area and is higher up.

00:46:27.610 --> 00:46:30.100
So I think these are part and parcel

00:46:30.100 --> 00:46:32.640
of the same kind of project.

00:46:32.640 --> 00:46:35.170
And I do have a project,

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:38.010
a book coming out with a law professor

00:46:38.010 --> 00:46:41.540
at the University of
Denver, Justin Marceau,

00:46:41.540 --> 00:46:43.740
in which we talk about these parallels,

00:46:43.740 --> 00:46:45.300
it's called Carceral Logics,

00:46:45.300 --> 00:46:47.980
Human Incarceration and Animal Captivity

00:46:47.980 --> 00:46:49.080
That'll be out in May.

00:46:51.484 --> 00:46:53.933
- And Professor Gruen is,

00:46:55.860 --> 00:46:59.510
that question really
revealed, brought out the way

00:47:05.136 --> 00:47:08.610
that your talk clearly generalized to that

00:47:08.610 --> 00:47:11.560
in the way that your false
off-cycle position does as well.

00:47:13.193 --> 00:47:15.343
So let's go to an edgy question,

00:47:17.250 --> 00:47:19.833
Yale perpetuates carceral logics.

00:47:20.840 --> 00:47:24.090
How can we use methods
of non-reformist reform

00:47:24.090 --> 00:47:26.090
to challenge these (indistinct)?

00:47:26.090 --> 00:47:28.270
- Well, I think I need a
little bit more context

00:47:28.270 --> 00:47:32.500
for the specific ways
in which Yale does this.

00:47:32.500 --> 00:47:36.930
I think it's not actually just Yale,

00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:38.270
I mean, I do think our institution-

00:47:38.270 --> 00:47:43.270
- Institutions like carceral
logic has shoots through

00:47:43.420 --> 00:47:46.530
the institutions in which we do non-reform

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:48.570
in which we discuss these matters.

00:47:48.570 --> 00:47:49.737
- Perfect, that's so true.

00:47:49.737 --> 00:47:52.130
- And so how does one deal with that

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:54.340
kind of performative contradiction

00:47:54.340 --> 00:47:56.560
the aren't you a hypocrite..?

00:47:57.760 --> 00:47:58.593
- Right, yeah, no,

00:47:58.593 --> 00:48:00.830
and I think the performative
contradiction point

00:48:00.830 --> 00:48:02.190
is a really important one,

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:05.860
and I think that if you
go back to the origins

00:48:05.860 --> 00:48:10.150
of the notion of non-reformist reforms,

00:48:10.150 --> 00:48:12.960
it's in the context of
thinking about what to do,

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:14.800
can we make any small changes

00:48:14.800 --> 00:48:18.340
or do we have to wait for
the socialist revolution?

00:48:18.340 --> 00:48:23.340
I think that the idea of
non-reformist reforms is that,

00:48:25.445 --> 00:48:27.210
and the reason I like using mutual aid

00:48:27.210 --> 00:48:29.593
as a really important example of that

00:48:29.593 --> 00:48:34.500
is that you don't go to the
system to ask the system

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:38.070
to help with the problem
because the system is designed

00:48:38.070 --> 00:48:40.780
to make it so you need help
with the problem, right?

00:48:40.780 --> 00:48:44.660
So, and this is part of the idea is that

00:48:44.660 --> 00:48:46.960
we know people say the
prison system is broken.

00:48:46.960 --> 00:48:48.910
No, the prison system is working

00:48:48.910 --> 00:48:51.210
exactly the way it was designed to work.

00:48:51.210 --> 00:48:54.530
So making modest reforms in that system

00:48:54.530 --> 00:48:57.510
is not going to do anything
to undo this system.

00:48:57.510 --> 00:49:00.900
And I think with every
institution, obviously,

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:02.750
this is part of what I was
saying at the beginning,

00:49:02.750 --> 00:49:04.440
we're in this position where

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:07.950
it is really hard to imagine
outside of these structures,

00:49:07.950 --> 00:49:10.120
I'm not pretending that
somehow philosophically

00:49:10.120 --> 00:49:13.620
I can just sort of will this
imagination into existence

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:15.863
that's not at all what I'm suggesting.

00:49:17.151 --> 00:49:19.070
It's a very, very, very hard project,

00:49:19.070 --> 00:49:21.610
but I do think there are various ways

00:49:21.610 --> 00:49:25.840
that we can develop
different social relations

00:49:25.840 --> 00:49:30.840
and we can think about it in
terms of there's movement afoot

00:49:31.450 --> 00:49:34.210
around getting rid of grades or example,

00:49:34.210 --> 00:49:35.100
that would be something

00:49:35.100 --> 00:49:37.350
that would be a non-reformist reform.

00:49:37.350 --> 00:49:38.480
You could still go to school,

00:49:38.480 --> 00:49:40.550
but you argue that we don't want grades

00:49:42.472 --> 00:49:44.210
those are the kinds of things that I think

00:49:44.210 --> 00:49:45.043
people can do.

00:49:45.043 --> 00:49:47.410
Ultimately these aren't gonna be top-down

00:49:47.410 --> 00:49:49.000
sort of changes either.

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:53.540
What you want to have is
in a community, in a group,

00:49:53.540 --> 00:49:55.950
hopefully an inclusive group was multiple

00:49:57.723 --> 00:49:59.880
people who are in multiple social

00:49:59.880 --> 00:50:02.290
and political locations working together.

00:50:02.290 --> 00:50:05.290
And I know that there are sort
of all sorts of activities

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:08.064
at Yale that are like that.

00:50:08.064 --> 00:50:09.870
There are lots and lots of folks

00:50:09.870 --> 00:50:12.120
who are doing really important

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:15.463
non-reformist reform work there and work.

00:50:16.870 --> 00:50:20.120
- Just a codicil to the points you made.

00:50:20.120 --> 00:50:23.440
Like Rod Fergusson points
out that the purpose

00:50:23.440 --> 00:50:28.440
of like the university ombudsman
is to people to complain

00:50:30.180 --> 00:50:33.650
is to undercut any student
movement that might occur,

00:50:33.650 --> 00:50:37.340
to get people to complain
to the institution itself

00:50:37.340 --> 00:50:38.800
to fix the problem.

00:50:38.800 --> 00:50:41.640
So then they can make minor reforms

00:50:41.640 --> 00:50:44.030
without challenging
the institution itself,

00:50:44.030 --> 00:50:48.480
to make the institution itself
the place where you complain

00:50:48.480 --> 00:50:50.410
to make sure that the institution

00:50:50.410 --> 00:50:53.130
is always the logic governing everything.

00:50:53.130 --> 00:50:54.110
- That's a good codicil.

00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:55.477
Another way of thinking about that

00:50:55.477 --> 00:50:59.313
and more pertinent in
my world lately is that,

00:51:01.100 --> 00:51:05.470
the faculty might organize for
better benefits or better pay

00:51:05.470 --> 00:51:08.700
and the university in sort
of endless certain members

00:51:08.700 --> 00:51:09.940
of the faculty and says,

00:51:09.940 --> 00:51:13.090
okay, let's give you better
benefits and you better pay,

00:51:13.090 --> 00:51:16.050
or let's give everybody a
little bit of a benefit,

00:51:16.050 --> 00:51:17.390
but not the full benefit.

00:51:17.390 --> 00:51:20.220
It's sort of co-opting the problem,

00:51:20.220 --> 00:51:23.470
and that was the exact set of debates

00:51:23.470 --> 00:51:25.240
that went on between revolution

00:51:25.240 --> 00:51:28.130
and reform in the early moment
when people were arguing

00:51:28.130 --> 00:51:30.160
in favor of non-reformist reforms.

00:51:30.160 --> 00:51:31.560
A really important question.

00:51:32.481 --> 00:51:33.320
- Right, so, right.

00:51:33.320 --> 00:51:38.003
So there's this structure
to institutional abolition

00:51:40.097 --> 00:51:42.497
and we can think of
institutional abolition say,

00:51:44.190 --> 00:51:47.490
dramatic generally, and
then there's a structure

00:51:47.490 --> 00:51:52.460
to counter abolition which is
to allow incremental reforms.

00:51:52.460 --> 00:51:53.293
Yeah.

00:51:53.293 --> 00:51:54.400
- That's right.

00:51:54.400 --> 00:51:58.773
So Jennifer Klein asks,

00:51:59.730 --> 00:52:04.730
so, my distinguish to our
labor historian here at Yale.

00:52:05.720 --> 00:52:08.640
To use ethics of care as
a foundation for abolition

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:12.210
do we need to also question

00:52:12.210 --> 00:52:14.270
the premise of liberal citizenship

00:52:14.270 --> 00:52:16.930
that fetishizes independence,

00:52:16.930 --> 00:52:18.980
cares about relations oof interdependence

00:52:20.634 --> 00:52:23.320
in our political culture
to stains and stigmatizes

00:52:23.320 --> 00:52:26.080
anything that can be cast as dependence.

00:52:26.080 --> 00:52:29.000
So how do we reconcile
dominance citizenship ideas

00:52:29.000 --> 00:52:32.200
of the model you present or change them?

00:52:32.200 --> 00:52:35.320
Also, kathi weeks
discusses utopian thinking

00:52:35.320 --> 00:52:38.210
not as having to be fully
formed rather as bridge

00:52:38.210 --> 00:52:40.910
to the alternative structure we want.

00:52:40.910 --> 00:52:41.930
- Yes. Yes.

00:52:41.930 --> 00:52:44.220
Great questions, super questions.

00:52:44.220 --> 00:52:46.990
I highly recommend the Care Manifesto

00:52:46.990 --> 00:52:50.340
that came out last year
by the Care Collective.

00:52:50.340 --> 00:52:53.160
And I think it does really
think that we need it,

00:52:53.160 --> 00:52:54.890
if you're really gonna
think about abolition

00:52:54.890 --> 00:52:58.710
in a full blown way, which I
did not do in this talk at all,

00:52:58.710 --> 00:53:01.010
but if we're gonna think
about it in a full-blown way,

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:04.170
we are absolutely going
to have to reconsider

00:53:04.170 --> 00:53:09.170
the kind of liberal citizen
formation that exists.

00:53:09.760 --> 00:53:12.873
And I think that there are
different forms of care ethics.

00:53:14.230 --> 00:53:17.640
My idea of care ethics is not one

00:53:17.640 --> 00:53:21.483
in which you can internalize
or the system can co-op care.

00:53:23.300 --> 00:53:24.610
So sort of basically pay for care

00:53:24.610 --> 00:53:28.120
or even make it so that
it's part of the system.

00:53:28.120 --> 00:53:29.530
That's absolutely something

00:53:29.530 --> 00:53:31.950
that we see happening all around,

00:53:31.950 --> 00:53:35.023
particularly vivid in the pandemic times.

00:53:36.336 --> 00:53:37.460
So I think that it's important

00:53:37.460 --> 00:53:39.510
to think about an ethics of care

00:53:39.510 --> 00:53:42.757
as a much more fundamentally
relational idea.

00:53:46.010 --> 00:53:48.010
It isn't going to be an idea

00:53:48.010 --> 00:53:52.300
that's based on independence
or individualism

00:53:53.300 --> 00:53:57.533
or any of those kinds of
distinguishing detaching notions.

00:54:00.608 --> 00:54:02.968
It's a fundamentally and I think the quote

00:54:02.968 --> 00:54:05.910
from Sarah Modest is
making a point of this,

00:54:05.910 --> 00:54:10.910
that the self is in inextricable
relation with others,

00:54:11.480 --> 00:54:15.130
that this isn't what I call
entangled relation with others.

00:54:15.130 --> 00:54:20.130
And these aren't things
to try to disentangle,

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:22.430
but to try to navigate,

00:54:22.430 --> 00:54:25.310
to negotiate and to
build better relations.

00:54:25.310 --> 00:54:29.050
And so I think absolutely
in the political realm

00:54:29.050 --> 00:54:33.130
there will need to be a
very important discussion.

00:54:33.130 --> 00:54:35.650
And there have there have
been people doing this work,

00:54:35.650 --> 00:54:39.040
Joan Tronto's another who's
done really important work

00:54:39.040 --> 00:54:43.470
in rethinking how care
can fit into rearrangement

00:54:43.470 --> 00:54:44.983
of political relations.

00:54:49.650 --> 00:54:51.010
- I'm still muted,

00:54:51.010 --> 00:54:54.500
lemme turn now to a student in the class

00:54:54.500 --> 00:54:56.100
who has two questions.

00:54:56.100 --> 00:55:00.320
I'll start with the first and
then go to another question.

00:55:00.320 --> 00:55:05.320
So the first is about the
reproduction of carceral logic

00:55:07.330 --> 00:55:08.663
in cancel culture.

00:55:10.897 --> 00:55:11.997
I'm gonna had to this,

00:55:15.250 --> 00:55:18.110
so the argument for this statement
follows as cancel culture

00:55:18.110 --> 00:55:20.870
is not focused on teaching
people the error of their ways,

00:55:20.870 --> 00:55:24.270
just places them outside
the circle of public view

00:55:24.270 --> 00:55:27.720
in a similar way to how prisons
label people as problems

00:55:27.720 --> 00:55:30.100
and sequester them from society.

00:55:30.100 --> 00:55:32.909
I'd love to hear your thoughts about this.

00:55:32.909 --> 00:55:34.250
- I think that's an excellent question.

00:55:34.250 --> 00:55:35.700
And I think that's exactly right.

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:37.960
I think that there is a carceral logic

00:55:37.960 --> 00:55:40.370
that's operating in cancel culture.

00:55:40.370 --> 00:55:42.270
I think too though, importantly,

00:55:42.270 --> 00:55:44.600
and this is what I was trying
to get at when I didn't,

00:55:44.600 --> 00:55:47.820
again, not didn't have time
to fully develop the idea,

00:55:47.820 --> 00:55:49.650
but there is a kind of effective

00:55:50.640 --> 00:55:53.710
longing as it were for
something to go right,

00:55:53.710 --> 00:55:57.440
and I think that presumably
there's an important way

00:55:57.440 --> 00:56:01.770
in which what we should be
recognizing in cancel culture

00:56:02.630 --> 00:56:07.630
is that there's already
something sort of disturbed

00:56:09.489 --> 00:56:14.310
in our thinking that makes us
want to cancel someone else.

00:56:14.310 --> 00:56:15.440
Now that isn't to say,

00:56:15.440 --> 00:56:19.490
so, I think I don't wanna in any way

00:56:19.490 --> 00:56:22.020
sort of overly psychologize that longing,

00:56:22.020 --> 00:56:26.790
I think it speaks to the ways

00:56:29.410 --> 00:56:34.410
that our social relations
are shallow or not robust,

00:56:38.170 --> 00:56:42.290
or there's something missing
in our social relations.

00:56:42.290 --> 00:56:45.620
And it's in virtue of that that we develop

00:56:45.620 --> 00:56:47.600
these kinds of us at them good,

00:56:47.600 --> 00:56:51.090
like you guys are the good
guys and we're the bad guys.

00:56:51.090 --> 00:56:56.090
And I think you talk about
this too in your fascism book,

00:56:56.400 --> 00:57:01.400
but there's this us them thing
has a certain kind of hold

00:57:03.790 --> 00:57:05.890
on us when we live in a broken state.

00:57:05.890 --> 00:57:08.950
I guess that's what I say
when I say a broken society,

00:57:08.950 --> 00:57:12.140
when our fundamental humanity

00:57:12.140 --> 00:57:15.550
and our fundamental animality
isn't being addressed

00:57:15.550 --> 00:57:18.540
or isn't being attended
to, and that developed.

00:57:18.540 --> 00:57:21.970
So then one way to respond to that

00:57:21.970 --> 00:57:26.380
sort of social hollowness, if you will,

00:57:26.380 --> 00:57:28.260
is to sort of develop this.

00:57:28.260 --> 00:57:31.860
Okay, I'm gonna cancel you
or you have to be set aside.

00:57:31.860 --> 00:57:33.790
Now, I do wanna say this,

00:57:33.790 --> 00:57:35.640
this brings to mind a
really important point

00:57:35.640 --> 00:57:39.253
that I wanna make about
abolition more generally.

00:57:41.570 --> 00:57:45.000
I think it's also really
important to keep in mind

00:57:45.000 --> 00:57:48.160
and cancel culture is
somewhat connected to this,

00:57:48.160 --> 00:57:50.090
that there are actually people

00:57:50.090 --> 00:57:52.950
who we don't want to interact with.

00:57:52.950 --> 00:57:56.090
We don't want in our
communities, we don't want,

00:57:56.090 --> 00:57:57.750
and I'm not pretending that everybody

00:57:57.750 --> 00:58:01.020
is somebody that we could sort
of, that we should embrace.

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:03.870
So I wanna also acknowledge
this isn't Pollyanna.

00:58:03.870 --> 00:58:06.700
It might be utopian, but it's
a different kind of utopian.

00:58:06.700 --> 00:58:07.840
It's not Pollyannish.

00:58:07.840 --> 00:58:09.500
It's not like, oh, well
let's all get along.

00:58:09.500 --> 00:58:10.850
No, it's nothing like that.

00:58:17.580 --> 00:58:20.640
- So the next question from Joe Fischel

00:58:20.640 --> 00:58:25.640
is about the relationship
between carceral logic

00:58:27.730 --> 00:58:29.583
and punitive logic.

00:58:30.780 --> 00:58:32.597
And what do we do

00:58:39.500 --> 00:58:40.600
and then it shifts.

00:58:40.600 --> 00:58:43.600
So the first question is
about analytically separating

00:58:43.600 --> 00:58:48.440
carceral logic from punitive
logics or punitive imaginaries,

00:58:48.440 --> 00:58:52.380
and then Professor Fischel wonders,

00:58:52.380 --> 00:58:54.430
what do we do with sort of the desire

00:58:54.430 --> 00:58:56.343
for libidinized retribution?

00:58:57.202 --> 00:59:01.130
- Hmm, hmm, hmm, so, right.

00:59:01.130 --> 00:59:05.320
I'm not separating carceral
logics from punitive logics.

00:59:05.320 --> 00:59:08.830
There's a part of that punitive logic

00:59:08.830 --> 00:59:12.147
that is as part of the carceral,

00:59:13.370 --> 00:59:16.700
carceral logics subsumes that
retributives retributivists

00:59:18.650 --> 00:59:20.730
sort of instinct or impulse,

00:59:20.730 --> 00:59:25.070
and so then the question becomes,

00:59:25.070 --> 00:59:27.070
okay, so, there's a
distinction to be made there.

00:59:27.070 --> 00:59:29.090
I'm not sure it matters.

00:59:29.090 --> 00:59:32.370
I like to think, I mean,
I like to talk about it

00:59:32.370 --> 00:59:33.790
in terms of carceral logic

00:59:33.790 --> 00:59:36.790
precisely because of the point
that Professor Stanley made

00:59:36.790 --> 00:59:40.270
that I'm interested in
captivity in a general way,

00:59:40.270 --> 00:59:45.270
punishment itself is an
interesting separate question,

00:59:45.850 --> 00:59:50.180
but my interests are in
the captive in the carceral

00:59:50.180 --> 00:59:53.853
and punishment is a feature
of that whole system,

00:59:54.870 --> 00:59:57.463
but I think the idea of retributivism is,

01:00:00.280 --> 01:00:01.530
and the impulse towards it

01:00:01.530 --> 01:00:04.810
that feeling that I was evoking
at the beginning of my talk,

01:00:04.810 --> 01:00:07.710
where I encouraged us to be thinking about

01:00:07.710 --> 01:00:12.300
why it is that we want these
is particularly people in power

01:00:12.300 --> 01:00:15.963
who abuse their power to be punished.

01:00:17.010 --> 01:00:20.380
I think, again, I would just
go back to what I was saying

01:00:20.380 --> 01:00:23.030
that desire and I'm not
denying that it's there

01:00:23.030 --> 01:00:25.033
because it is there and I have it too,

01:00:26.250 --> 01:00:30.310
but that desire is a feature
of a distorted system, right?

01:00:30.310 --> 01:00:32.980
And I can't help but think
of Catharine MacKinnon

01:00:32.980 --> 01:00:35.030
whenever I talk about this, which is,

01:00:35.030 --> 01:00:38.300
essentially that we
can't really out-think,

01:00:38.300 --> 01:00:40.630
we can't think about
what it might be like.

01:00:40.630 --> 01:00:43.020
Catharine MacKinnon uses the sort of idea

01:00:43.020 --> 01:00:47.410
about sexual desire,
but the notion is like,

01:00:47.410 --> 01:00:50.250
we don't really know what it would be like

01:00:50.250 --> 01:00:54.310
to have genuine desire
outside of patriarchy

01:00:54.310 --> 01:00:55.690
because we live in patriarchy,

01:00:55.690 --> 01:00:57.300
and I think the same thing as something

01:00:57.300 --> 01:00:58.760
that I would wanna say,

01:00:58.760 --> 01:01:01.850
I think the desires that
we have for punishment

01:01:01.850 --> 01:01:06.260
and retribution are a feature
of the carceral system

01:01:06.260 --> 01:01:08.810
that we live in and the carceral logic

01:01:08.810 --> 01:01:10.810
that's colonized our thinking

01:01:10.810 --> 01:01:13.740
and that if we're able to get
beyond that carceral logic,

01:01:13.740 --> 01:01:15.480
and I hope we could do that

01:01:15.480 --> 01:01:19.080
through really thinking
and imagining otherwise

01:01:20.580 --> 01:01:23.610
through a kind of
abolitionist ethic of care

01:01:23.610 --> 01:01:26.040
that we might actually
be able to figure out

01:01:26.040 --> 01:01:28.587
that we're not committed to retributivism.

01:01:28.587 --> 01:01:30.780
And in philosophy and
the philosophy of law

01:01:30.780 --> 01:01:32.360
and philosophy of punishment

01:01:32.360 --> 01:01:33.550
there's been a lot of debates

01:01:33.550 --> 01:01:38.550
about sort of what is the
underlying moral, ethical,

01:01:39.220 --> 01:01:44.220
political values that are
achieved through punishment.

01:01:46.130 --> 01:01:49.100
And I think already this is a good example

01:01:49.100 --> 01:01:52.090
of something that you buying
into the carceral system.

01:01:52.090 --> 01:01:54.340
So we're gonna debate at the edges,

01:01:54.340 --> 01:01:55.610
should it be retributive,

01:01:55.610 --> 01:01:59.600
or should it be like we're
trying to deter people

01:01:59.600 --> 01:02:03.200
or is this just really a
utilitarian way of dealing with,

01:02:03.200 --> 01:02:05.210
but that already is within the system.

01:02:05.210 --> 01:02:08.520
And my view is that we really
need to challenge the system

01:02:08.520 --> 01:02:13.520
of thinking about why that's the way

01:02:13.820 --> 01:02:15.510
we wanna organize our lives.

01:02:15.510 --> 01:02:16.343
So for example,

01:02:16.343 --> 01:02:19.340
what would we do with somebody
who was really egregious,

01:02:19.340 --> 01:02:24.300
nobody could handle, I
dunno, put them just,

01:02:25.580 --> 01:02:29.230
turn your back on them or
don't let them into your home,

01:02:29.230 --> 01:02:30.710
or don't let them into your community,

01:02:30.710 --> 01:02:32.883
or don't let them into your space.

01:02:36.335 --> 01:02:39.853
- So, the way I understand
your response is,

01:02:40.790 --> 01:02:43.180
but the idea don't think of carceral logic

01:02:43.180 --> 01:02:46.730
as like a set of principles
think of it as an ideology

01:02:46.730 --> 01:02:49.660
where an ideology is principles,

01:02:49.660 --> 01:02:53.880
practices, and attitudes
and emotions to things.

01:02:53.880 --> 01:02:57.370
And you've got to eliminate
the entire ideology,

01:02:57.370 --> 01:03:01.810
the desire for retribution
as part of an ideology,

01:03:01.810 --> 01:03:04.363
an ideology is not just a logic.

01:03:05.500 --> 01:03:09.380
And hopefully if we get
rid of the structure,

01:03:09.380 --> 01:03:12.920
the hypothesis is we'll
get rid of those feelings

01:03:12.920 --> 01:03:16.550
of retribution, though it does seem to me

01:03:16.550 --> 01:03:18.510
that the feelings of retribution

01:03:19.940 --> 01:03:24.600
last through a number of
different forms of punishment,

01:03:24.600 --> 01:03:28.543
even before the prison say.

01:03:29.400 --> 01:03:30.950
The carceral logic you're trying

01:03:30.950 --> 01:03:34.040
to get rid of is much
deeper than just the prison.

01:03:34.040 --> 01:03:36.240
- Yeah, yeah, that's right.

01:03:37.220 --> 01:03:40.300
Shelly asks about how we broaden this.

01:03:40.300 --> 01:03:43.810
So professor is a very
distinguished philosopher

01:03:43.810 --> 01:03:47.620
of disability asks,

01:03:47.620 --> 01:03:52.530
how we include carceral
logics and abolition,

01:03:52.530 --> 01:03:55.430
how we broaden that to
include institutions

01:03:55.430 --> 01:03:58.450
and social phenomenons such
as nursing homes, psych wards,

01:03:58.450 --> 01:04:00.693
group homes for disabled
people, et cetera?

01:04:01.650 --> 01:04:06.650
- Well, that's, I mean, I put
them in that list earlier,

01:04:06.800 --> 01:04:10.680
Shelly, professor partly because
you've done such important

01:04:10.680 --> 01:04:12.740
and interesting work
and continue to do work

01:04:12.740 --> 01:04:17.150
particularly through the
pandemic on these institutions,

01:04:17.150 --> 01:04:19.460
but that's partly why
it's really important

01:04:19.460 --> 01:04:22.720
to think about this as
just as professor Stanley

01:04:22.720 --> 01:04:24.660
was just saying as an ideology

01:04:24.660 --> 01:04:27.830
or as I'm thinking of
it an overarching logic

01:04:27.830 --> 01:04:32.010
that applies beyond the prison
situation and beyond the zoo.

01:04:32.010 --> 01:04:35.140
I think, I mean, we
really need to think about

01:04:35.140 --> 01:04:40.140
those as canonical cases, but
there are many, many places

01:04:41.180 --> 01:04:46.180
in which the structure
is designed precisely

01:04:48.180 --> 01:04:53.180
to hold on to those that are
within it in a diminished way,

01:04:58.230 --> 01:05:00.770
the institutions require that.

01:05:00.770 --> 01:05:05.290
There's also some really important work

01:05:05.290 --> 01:05:10.113
on deinstitutionalization that's been done

01:05:12.210 --> 01:05:17.210
that is particularly in the
area of psychiatric institutions

01:05:18.260 --> 01:05:21.980
that also takes an
abolition in a very special

01:05:21.980 --> 01:05:23.980
and important direction.

01:05:23.980 --> 01:05:26.700
So obviously I couldn't
speak to all of these issues,

01:05:26.700 --> 01:05:28.050
including the animal issue

01:05:28.050 --> 01:05:31.500
that is sort of close to my heart tonight,

01:05:31.500 --> 01:05:33.923
but thank you for raising that Shelly.

01:05:34.930 --> 01:05:37.380
- I'm gonna put myself in the queue

01:05:37.380 --> 01:05:40.810
even though that's a slight
violation of decorum.

01:05:40.810 --> 01:05:44.170
So I wanted to go back to
Professor Klein's question

01:05:44.170 --> 01:05:45.650
about liberal citizenship

01:05:46.821 --> 01:05:51.510
and ask a sort of maybe devil's
advocate kind of question

01:05:51.510 --> 01:05:55.370
about the ethics of care
background to your work,

01:05:55.370 --> 01:05:59.240
which so there's a way of attacking,

01:05:59.240 --> 01:06:02.440
there's a way of going at this

01:06:03.710 --> 01:06:05.740
which leans on liberal citizenship,

01:06:05.740 --> 01:06:09.540
which says that what's
happening in prisons

01:06:09.540 --> 01:06:12.663
is a violation of equality of citizenship.

01:06:13.510 --> 01:06:15.400
And when you're asking people

01:06:15.400 --> 01:06:18.499
to be responsible for their actions,

01:06:18.499 --> 01:06:21.920
your considering them as equal citizens,

01:06:21.920 --> 01:06:26.340
this is the kind of
thing that Douglas says

01:06:26.340 --> 01:06:28.070
and what the slave is the 4th of July.

01:06:28.070 --> 01:06:30.280
It's like, you're not dehumanizing us

01:06:30.280 --> 01:06:33.770
because you're treating us as
responsible for our actions,

01:06:33.770 --> 01:06:38.770
but then this method of treating
it undercuts the equality

01:06:40.550 --> 01:06:45.550
of citizenship that taking us
as responsible for our actions

01:06:46.300 --> 01:06:47.803
seems to presuppose,

01:06:49.100 --> 01:06:50.990
but all of that is within the framework

01:06:50.990 --> 01:06:52.163
of liberal citizenship.

01:06:58.950 --> 01:07:00.263
The way I think of it,

01:07:01.240 --> 01:07:04.630
one kind of reasoning I'm attracted to is,

01:07:04.630 --> 01:07:07.980
well, this kind of treatment
of a fellow citizens

01:07:07.980 --> 01:07:11.130
is incompatible with
treating them as responsible

01:07:11.130 --> 01:07:15.260
for their actions, treating
them as equal citizens

01:07:15.260 --> 01:07:18.580
in this more full blooded liberal sense.

01:07:18.580 --> 01:07:20.483
What survives from that?

01:07:21.909 --> 01:07:23.110
And that's a very different basis.

01:07:23.110 --> 01:07:24.850
One that doesn't generalize

01:07:25.860 --> 01:07:29.870
as much as an ethics of care view does

01:07:29.870 --> 01:07:31.933
to say captivity of animals.

01:07:32.980 --> 01:07:34.940
- Well, okay, so there's a lot in that,

01:07:34.940 --> 01:07:37.630
and if I'm understanding, I mean,

01:07:37.630 --> 01:07:42.630
I think that the problem with
the liberal citizenship model

01:07:43.410 --> 01:07:47.170
is it, I mean, buys into,

01:07:47.170 --> 01:07:52.170
or it's presupposes a very
specific form of agency,

01:07:54.560 --> 01:07:57.300
a very specific form of responsibility,

01:07:57.300 --> 01:08:01.180
a very specific form of identity

01:08:01.180 --> 01:08:05.820
that is part of upholding
the liberal framework.

01:08:05.820 --> 01:08:10.820
So it has this way in
which we have a framework

01:08:19.160 --> 01:08:23.987
that imagines us as sort
of individuals outside

01:08:25.030 --> 01:08:30.017
of social political,
familial, intimate relations.

01:08:31.200 --> 01:08:33.910
And then it creates the conditions

01:08:33.910 --> 01:08:38.480
for making it so that if
we don't do something,

01:08:38.480 --> 01:08:41.780
it looks like our freedom
or agency is being violated.

01:08:41.780 --> 01:08:43.180
I'm not making...

01:08:43.180 --> 01:08:45.930
What I'm trying to
suggest is that sort of,

01:08:45.930 --> 01:08:47.670
there's a self fulfilling

01:08:47.670 --> 01:08:51.250
and a self-sustaining part
of liberal citizenship

01:08:51.250 --> 01:08:55.170
that makes it seem and it's
not just liberal citizenship,

01:08:55.170 --> 01:08:56.130
it's current.

01:08:56.130 --> 01:09:00.220
I mean, it's actually, it
goes farther back in a sense,

01:09:00.220 --> 01:09:01.960
so that there's this idea that

01:09:01.960 --> 01:09:03.980
what it means to be responsible,

01:09:03.980 --> 01:09:06.670
what it means to be an agent,

01:09:06.670 --> 01:09:11.670
what it means to be a member
of a functioning ethical world,

01:09:16.880 --> 01:09:19.490
is that I have value,

01:09:19.490 --> 01:09:24.130
my value is tied to myself
as recognizing that value

01:09:24.130 --> 01:09:28.290
as Professor Chris Gorse would
say, it's tethered to me.

01:09:28.290 --> 01:09:31.130
And I wanna go back
farther than that and say,

01:09:31.130 --> 01:09:36.063
no, that presupposition that
we somehow sort of appear

01:09:37.060 --> 01:09:41.130
as individuals fully
formed is just a fiction,

01:09:41.130 --> 01:09:42.680
and that fiction-

01:09:42.680 --> 01:09:45.450
- I got that what you want to do,

01:09:45.450 --> 01:09:48.690
but it doesn't seem, I mean,
I think in other words,

01:09:48.690 --> 01:09:52.730
you can have a conscience
abolitionists program though,

01:09:52.730 --> 01:09:55.387
I mean, you haven't argued
that you can, right?

01:09:58.280 --> 01:09:59.240
But you've present,

01:09:59.240 --> 01:10:02.220
but abolition and the
ethics of caring framework

01:10:02.220 --> 01:10:05.563
is a much more philosophically
radical program.

01:10:06.950 --> 01:10:11.700
Then you could imagine an
abolitionist program extending

01:10:11.700 --> 01:10:13.310
from abolition of slavery

01:10:13.310 --> 01:10:15.950
that's ideologically consistent with it

01:10:15.950 --> 01:10:18.440
that remains within the liberal tradition.

01:10:18.440 --> 01:10:19.560
- Right, right, right, right.

01:10:19.560 --> 01:10:21.960
No, I think that, I mean,
that's a really important point,

01:10:21.960 --> 01:10:26.320
and I think that I wasn't
arguing actually in in that vein,

01:10:26.320 --> 01:10:29.193
I do think that there's a sense, however,

01:10:31.050 --> 01:10:34.700
that we can get in
trouble at some juncture

01:10:34.700 --> 01:10:37.090
if we go that route

01:10:37.090 --> 01:10:40.270
and the trouble is that
we're reinforcing the system

01:10:40.270 --> 01:10:41.570
that's built on,

01:10:41.570 --> 01:10:44.500
I mean, carceral logics
come out of that system,

01:10:44.500 --> 01:10:47.350
and so I don't know how
that system can survive

01:10:47.350 --> 01:10:50.300
without carceral logic, but
it's an important point,

01:10:50.300 --> 01:10:52.710
and I should probably think about,

01:10:52.710 --> 01:10:54.980
it's a very important point
that I should probably think

01:10:54.980 --> 01:10:57.750
about the ways in which
I didn't argue this,

01:10:57.750 --> 01:11:00.150
but I think there is an
important sense in which

01:11:02.786 --> 01:11:05.940
the sort of Genesis as it were,

01:11:05.940 --> 01:11:08.380
the relationship of the
Genesis of the carceral logic

01:11:08.380 --> 01:11:12.190
and this liberal citizen
notion is actually

01:11:13.150 --> 01:11:16.840
I think worth my sort of
cashing out a little bit.

01:11:16.840 --> 01:11:17.673
Thank you.

01:11:17.673 --> 01:11:18.506
- Yeah, I mean, Murakawa

01:11:18.506 --> 01:11:21.710
I think tries to argue
that liberalism results

01:11:21.710 --> 01:11:25.780
in the carceral logic,
but I don't think it's,

01:11:25.780 --> 01:11:28.580
I love Naomi Murakawa's
work, but I don't think,

01:11:28.580 --> 01:11:31.030
I'm not completely convinced that

01:11:31.030 --> 01:11:33.480
that it's not a contingent
fact of history,

01:11:33.480 --> 01:11:36.390
what Murakawa describes and...

01:11:42.510 --> 01:11:44.593
Okay, lemme go two more questions.

01:11:47.395 --> 01:11:48.228
So, let's see.

01:11:51.016 --> 01:11:53.076
Oh, I mean, oh, wow. Oh, sorry.

01:11:53.076 --> 01:11:54.130
I was on the, okay.

01:11:54.130 --> 01:11:56.013
Open, ah, here we are.

01:12:05.310 --> 01:12:07.667
Lemme just look to see, right.

01:12:16.750 --> 01:12:21.750
Right, Professor Klein
weighs in to point out that

01:12:21.920 --> 01:12:23.810
the people who develop the asylum

01:12:23.810 --> 01:12:27.150
and the penitentiary saw them as involved

01:12:27.150 --> 01:12:28.393
in a project of care.

01:12:29.270 --> 01:12:31.220
Right, and that's definitely one thing

01:12:31.220 --> 01:12:34.650
even solitary confinement was envisaged

01:12:34.650 --> 01:12:36.610
in Eastern State Penitentiary that way.

01:12:36.610 --> 01:12:40.820
- Yeah, right. That's right, that's right,

01:12:40.820 --> 01:12:42.653
but I think that it's important.

01:12:45.734 --> 01:12:48.600
I mean, I think that
these notions of care,

01:12:48.600 --> 01:12:53.500
I think the idea of care
is not just, I care,

01:12:53.500 --> 01:12:58.500
so I'm gonna say, early
forms of solitary confinement

01:13:00.090 --> 01:13:03.670
as Lisa Guenther so recently points out,

01:13:03.670 --> 01:13:05.940
we're designed precisely
to care for the person

01:13:05.940 --> 01:13:07.060
who's gone astray

01:13:07.060 --> 01:13:08.967
and allow them time to
reflect on themselves

01:13:08.967 --> 01:13:12.150
and to develop sort of a quiet mind,

01:13:12.150 --> 01:13:17.150
so that they can move in a
very more productive direction.

01:13:19.590 --> 01:13:21.070
And I think that that's partly,

01:13:21.070 --> 01:13:23.980
I hinted at this care
as I'm understanding it

01:13:23.980 --> 01:13:27.210
is not that sort of
condescending paternalistic

01:13:27.210 --> 01:13:29.250
pitying sort of notion.

01:13:29.250 --> 01:13:33.400
And there is I think important
debate amongst care theorists

01:13:33.400 --> 01:13:37.320
about what sorts of care
we're talking about.

01:13:37.320 --> 01:13:40.330
And there is just to go
back to your earlier point,

01:13:40.330 --> 01:13:43.590
there's a very rich tradition
of liberal care theorists

01:13:43.590 --> 01:13:47.760
that are developing views about
when can care be monetized,

01:13:47.760 --> 01:13:49.900
when can it be sort of incorporated

01:13:49.900 --> 01:13:53.739
into a sort of financial exchange,

01:13:53.739 --> 01:13:54.820
and those sorts of questions.

01:13:54.820 --> 01:13:56.440
That isn't the care I'm thinking about,

01:13:56.440 --> 01:13:58.093
but of course it exists, yes.

01:14:00.310 --> 01:14:01.280
- Professor Klein notes

01:14:01.280 --> 01:14:05.810
on anti-hierarchical
form of collective care

01:14:05.810 --> 01:14:06.643
is what we need.

01:14:08.708 --> 01:14:10.808
So, again, another question from a student

01:14:15.210 --> 01:14:17.580
who wants to hear about your thoughts

01:14:17.580 --> 01:14:21.250
on endowment justice
activism, and in particular,

01:14:21.250 --> 01:14:24.533
how it measures up as a kind
of non-reformist reform?

01:14:27.831 --> 01:14:29.910
- So I think this is a really exciting

01:14:29.910 --> 01:14:33.590
and interesting question and thinking.

01:14:33.590 --> 01:14:35.550
I would like to hear a little bit more

01:14:35.550 --> 01:14:36.820
about this kind of activism,

01:14:36.820 --> 01:14:39.860
but if I'm thinking of
what it triggers for me,

01:14:39.860 --> 01:14:43.683
it's this idea that somehow this whole,

01:14:44.950 --> 01:14:47.240
well, maybe you can tell me

01:14:47.240 --> 01:14:49.523
before I start launching into this.

01:14:51.117 --> 01:14:55.000
- So, this is like asking the university

01:14:55.000 --> 01:14:57.820
to no longer be invested in fossil fuels.

01:14:57.820 --> 01:14:59.318
- Okay, I just wanted to make sure

01:14:59.318 --> 01:15:00.570
that's what I was thinking,
but then I thought, wait,

01:15:00.570 --> 01:15:01.710
maybe there's some other thing here.

01:15:01.710 --> 01:15:06.693
Right, so, I mean, I think
these kinds of things aren't,

01:15:09.468 --> 01:15:11.937
I don't know if they are non-reformist.

01:15:13.820 --> 01:15:18.360
I think ultimately, and this is tricky,

01:15:18.360 --> 01:15:22.670
but ultimately sort of
moving around capital

01:15:22.670 --> 01:15:26.760
from one thing to another
holds onto the structure.

01:15:26.760 --> 01:15:31.760
And so my sense is that just as divestment

01:15:32.140 --> 01:15:34.980
in the area of fossil fuels for example

01:15:36.960 --> 01:15:41.400
or techno solutions to climate change,

01:15:41.400 --> 01:15:44.500
sort of all of those things are part of,

01:15:44.500 --> 01:15:49.500
are just another form of sort
of maintaining the system.

01:15:51.338 --> 01:15:53.470
I know that's a harsh thing to say,

01:15:53.470 --> 01:15:55.080
but I think if you think about

01:15:55.080 --> 01:15:58.560
non-reformist reforms seriously,

01:15:58.560 --> 01:16:01.410
it really doesn't mean
that you can move money

01:16:01.410 --> 01:16:05.290
from one drawer to the
other, that's to reformist.

01:16:05.290 --> 01:16:06.840
It's not non-reformist.

01:16:06.840 --> 01:16:10.210
What you would need to do is
take the money from that drawer

01:16:10.210 --> 01:16:12.890
and give it to the people
who don't have any money

01:16:12.890 --> 01:16:14.270
that would be non-reformist,

01:16:14.270 --> 01:16:15.383
but that's not what we're talking about

01:16:15.383 --> 01:16:17.500
when we're talking about divesting.

01:16:17.500 --> 01:16:20.720
So I think that's just a reform,

01:16:20.720 --> 01:16:22.453
it's not a non-reformist reform.

01:16:27.290 --> 01:16:31.440
- Yeah, sometimes I worry
that the kinds of reforms

01:16:31.440 --> 01:16:36.410
we ourselves get involved
in was wishful thinking

01:16:36.410 --> 01:16:38.420
that their non-performance reforms,

01:16:38.420 --> 01:16:41.170
but that's just simply how life is.

01:16:41.170 --> 01:16:43.720
- Right and that's the, I
mean, I do wanna just say

01:16:44.894 --> 01:16:46.100
this is hard.

01:16:46.100 --> 01:16:48.060
I mean, this is hard intellectually,

01:16:48.060 --> 01:16:50.380
this is hard emotionally,

01:16:50.380 --> 01:16:53.910
this is hard to do in the system

01:16:55.530 --> 01:16:58.150
and there really is no
such thing as clean hands,

01:16:58.150 --> 01:16:59.700
no such thing as being pure,

01:16:59.700 --> 01:17:02.490
no such thing as being righteous.

01:17:02.490 --> 01:17:04.290
You do what you can do.

01:17:04.290 --> 01:17:08.210
- Yeah, and as Kristie Dotson said

01:17:08.210 --> 01:17:09.820
in that conference ran,

01:17:09.820 --> 01:17:12.443
injustice means that
all the options are bad.

01:17:15.436 --> 01:17:19.000
So, Anna Marie asked a
really interesting question

01:17:20.290 --> 01:17:22.860
about the functioning of
a criminal court system

01:17:22.860 --> 01:17:24.360
in abolishing carceral logics.

01:17:27.430 --> 01:17:29.990
And also relatedly,

01:17:29.990 --> 01:17:32.260
is there a difference
in wanting a conviction

01:17:32.260 --> 01:17:35.510
for the purpose of society's
disapproval of certain actions

01:17:35.510 --> 01:17:38.380
and not necessarily wanting incarceration?

01:17:38.380 --> 01:17:40.680
- Yeah, so I think these are two

01:17:40.680 --> 01:17:41.730
really interesting questions.

01:17:41.730 --> 01:17:44.090
I don't think that the
criminal court system

01:17:44.090 --> 01:17:48.240
that we have ever known is non-carceral.

01:17:48.240 --> 01:17:50.750
In fact, it is carceral
logics that organizes

01:17:50.750 --> 01:17:52.780
whichever system those might be.

01:17:52.780 --> 01:17:54.600
And there was early systems

01:17:54.600 --> 01:17:56.380
for those of you who are
interested in animals,

01:17:56.380 --> 01:17:59.780
there were early systems where
animals were put on trial

01:17:59.780 --> 01:18:01.410
for doing various things,

01:18:01.410 --> 01:18:03.330
because animals were thought to have

01:18:03.330 --> 01:18:05.183
agency and responsibility,

01:18:06.080 --> 01:18:09.100
but criminal court systems are designed,

01:18:09.100 --> 01:18:11.380
are sort of a feature of the..

01:18:12.220 --> 01:18:15.160
Carceral logics organizes
all criminal court systems.

01:18:15.160 --> 01:18:16.440
So it's not clear to me

01:18:16.440 --> 01:18:21.390
that you could have a non-carceral
criminal court system.

01:18:21.390 --> 01:18:23.260
The question, the second question though,

01:18:23.260 --> 01:18:24.570
is a question about

01:18:25.920 --> 01:18:30.920
maybe wanting some sort of acknowledgement

01:18:31.250 --> 01:18:32.700
or accountability is that,

01:18:32.700 --> 01:18:36.050
am I hearing that right
without having punishment?

01:18:36.050 --> 01:18:39.070
And that is certainly the
project of many people

01:18:39.070 --> 01:18:41.320
who are working in
restorative justice work

01:18:41.320 --> 01:18:43.010
and transformative justice work.

01:18:43.010 --> 01:18:46.340
The idea that what we want,

01:18:46.340 --> 01:18:48.570
it's not that everybody
can do whatever they want

01:18:48.570 --> 01:18:51.650
and there's no consequences
and nobody's gonna be upset,

01:18:51.650 --> 01:18:53.470
nobody's going to be hurt.

01:18:53.470 --> 01:18:58.470
That's the kind of utopianism
that I really don't like.

01:19:00.310 --> 01:19:04.900
It's the idea instead is
that when people do harm,

01:19:04.900 --> 01:19:09.160
when people cause harm, when
people bring about harm,

01:19:09.160 --> 01:19:12.480
the way to address that
is not through a system

01:19:12.480 --> 01:19:17.480
of a punitive system, a
prison system, a legal system,

01:19:17.890 --> 01:19:20.943
it's through a system of
community accountability.

01:19:21.960 --> 01:19:24.610
And even there, that's
what I was saying earlier.

